\begin{abstract}
In data stream clustering, systematic theory of stream clustering algorithms remains relatively scarce. Recently, density-based methods have gained attention. However, existing algorithms struggle to simultaneously handle arbitrarily shaped, multi-density, high-dimensional data while maintaining strong outlier resistance. Clustering quality significantly deteriorates when data density varies complexly. This paper proposes a clustering algorithm based on the novel concept of Tightest Neighbors and introduces a data stream clustering theory based on the Skeleton Set. Based on these theories, this paper develops a new method, TNStream, a fully online algorithm. The algorithm adaptively determines the clustering radius based on local similarity, summarizing the evolution of multi-density data streams in micro-clusters. It then applies a Tightest Neighbors-based clustering algorithm to form final clusters. To improve efficiency in high-dimensional cases, Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH) is employed to structure micro-clusters, addressing the challenge of storing $k$-nearest neighbors. TNStream is evaluated on various synthetic and real-world datasets using different clustering metrics. Experimental results demonstrate its effectiveness in improving clustering quality for multi-density data and validate the proposed data stream clustering theory.
%This study proposes a data stream clustering algorithm, TNStream, based on the Tightest Neighbors (TN) relationship. By constructing the Tightest Neighbors Graph (TNG), we investigate the relationship between the number of tightest neighbors and graph connectivity. Through the relationship between Tightest Neighborhood Closure Invariance and the multiplicity \( k \), we define the Tightest Neighborhood Closure Invariant Set and propose the \( k \)-TNC clustering algorithm by setting an appropriate \( k \). Theoretically, we introduce the Absolutely Distance Dividable (ADD) dataset and further propose the Connectedly Dividable (CD) dataset for cases with weaker conditions. By analyzing the fundamental principles of data stream clustering, we define Prototype Points as representatives of clusters and introduce the Skeleton Set as a structural determinant of data stream clustering. Utilizing KD-Tree, Ball-Tree, and LSH, we construct the Skeleton Set from micro-clusters and apply the \( k \)-TNC algorithm to form macro-clusters from the data stream. Experimental results demonstrate that TNStream effectively clusters arbitrarily shaped data, exhibits robustness to outliers, efficiently handles high-dimensional data, and achieves high-quality clustering within an acceptable runtime. Furthermore, it excels in clustering multi-density data, consistently outperforming traditional clustering algorithms in clustering quality. These findings further confirm the practical applicability and effectiveness of TNStream in dynamic data stream clustering analysis, marking a significant advancement in this field.
%本研究提出了一种基于Tightest Neighbors的数据流聚类算法——TNStream。基于新型近邻关系——Tightest Neighbors，我们构造Tightest Neighbors Graph，观察紧邻个数k与图连接性之间的关系，通过Tightest Neighborhood Closure Invariance和重数k之间的关系，提出紧邻闭包不变集，通过设置紧邻个数k，提出聚类算法kTNC。理论上，我们提出Absolutely Distance Dividable数据集，针对条件更弱的数据集，提出Connectedly Dividable数据集类型。我们通过剖析数据流聚类算法的底层逻辑，提出代表类簇的Prototype Point，以及决定数据流聚类的Skeleton Set。我们基于KD-Tree、Ball-Tree、LSH三种数据结构，通过微簇形成的Skeleton Set，通过kTNC对数据流进行聚类得到宏簇。实验验证表明，我们的算法可以定义任意形状的聚类，对异常值具有鲁棒性，能够处理高维数据，在可接受的运行时间内实现高质量的聚类，并且能够高效地聚类多密度数据。在聚类质量上均优于传统聚类算法。这些研究成果进一步证明了TNStream 算法在动态数据流聚类分析中的实际应用价值和有效性，为该领域带来了重要的进展。
