\section{Preliminaries}
\subsection{KD-Tree, Ball-Tree \& LSH data structure and range search}
%K-维树（KD-Tree）是一种高效的数据结构，专为处理高维数据集而设计，查询时间复杂度为 \( O(\log n) \)，广泛应用于许多领域（\textsuperscript{\cite{Bentley1975}）。它支持范围搜索，可在给定半径内快速查找数据点，且其运行时间复杂度为 \( O(dn^{1 - \frac{1}{d}} + k) \)，其中 \( d \) 是数据维度，\( n \) 是数据集大小，\( k \) 是查询结果数量（\textsuperscript{\cite{Agarwal2000}）。这种复杂度使得 KD-Tree 在大规模数据集上具有显著优势，尤其在多维数据分析中。如图 \ref{fig:kd} 所示，K-维树通过交替对 \( x \)-维度和 \( y \)-维度进行排序，确保高效的数据存储与检索，尤其在范围搜索时提供了显著的性能提升。
The KD-Tree is an efficient data structure designed for handling high-dimensional datasets, with a query time complexity of \( O(\log n) \), making it widely used in many fields \textsuperscript{\cite{Bentley1975}}. It supports range search, allowing for fast data point retrieval within a given radius, with a time complexity of \( O(dn^{1 - \frac{1}{d}} + k) \), where \( d \) is the data dimension, \( n \) is the dataset size, and \( k \) is the number of query results \textsuperscript{\cite{Agarwal2000}}. This complexity provides KD-Tree with a significant advantage when handling large-scale datasets, particularly in multidimensional data analysis. As shown in Figure \ref{fig:skd}, the KD-Tree ensures efficient data storage and retrieval by alternately sorting the data based on the \( x \)-dimension and \( y \)-dimension, offering significant performance improvements, especially in range searches.

The Ball-Tree organizes data by dividing the space into nested hyperspheres (balls), as opposed to hyperplanes used in other methods \textsuperscript{\cite{balltree1}}. Each node in a ball tree represents a ball that contains a set of points, described by its center and radius. In high-dimensional spaces, ball trees are typically more efficient than KD-Trees because they better exploit spatial locality by grouping points within the balls.

Finding nearest neighbors in high-dimensional spaces is crucial for multimedia retrieval, machine learning, and bioinformatics. In low-dimensional settings (dimensions <10), tree-based structures such as KD-Trees and Ball-Trees perform well. However, in high-dimensional spaces, they suffer from the “curse of dimensionality,” often performing worse than brute-force search. Approximate search methods address this issue by efficiently retrieving sufficiently close results when exact accuracy is unnecessary. Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH) is a leading approach for approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search. Compared to KD-Trees and Ball-Trees, LSH reduces the search space through dimensionality reduction, enhancing search efficiency \textsuperscript{\cite{Datar2004}}.

