\begin{definition}[$k$-Tightest Neighborhood Closure]
Assume that $A$ is a subset of $X$. We call the set
\[
cl_k(A)=\bigcup_{x\in A}TN(k,x) \tag{3.4}
\]
as the closure of $k$-tightest neighborhood of $A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$k$-Tightest Neighborhood Closure Invariance]
For some $k > 0$, if $cl_k(A)=A$, we say that the set $A$ is $k$-tightest neighborhood closure invariant. Obviously, $X$ always is $k$-tightest neighborhood closure invariant for all $k$. We say that it is trivial, otherwise, it is nontrivial.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Multiplicity]
For a given integer $k > 0$ and set $A\subseteq X$, we say that $\underbrace{cl_k(cl_k(\ldots cl_k(A)))}_{s}$ is $s$ multiple $k$-tightest neighborhood closure of $A$, denoted by $cl_k^s(A)$. Especially, when $A$ is a single point of $X$, we call $cl_k^s(A)$ as $s$ multiple $k$-tightest neighborhood closure of single point.
\end{definition}

The $k$-tightest neighborhood closure is the union of a set and the tightest neighbors of each point in the set. Experiments on a variety of different datasets show that the number of points in the set obtained by multiple iterations of the tightest neighborhood closure often does not change, and the obtained set often matches the true partition of the dataset itself. Therefore, we define the final set as the minimum $k$-tightest neighborhood closure invariant set ($k$-MTNCIS), and use it to mine the true partition information of the dataset itself.

\begin{definition}[$k$-MTNCIS]
For a given integer $k > 0$, a set $A\subset X$ is called as the minimum $k$-tightest neighborhood closure invariant set ($k$-MTNCIS), if
\begin{enumerate}
    \item the set $A$ is $k$-tightest neighborhood closure invariant; and
    \item for a sufficiently great number $s$, $cl_k^s(\{x_i\}) = A$ for any $x_i\in A$ holds.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{property}
Let set $A$ be $k$-MTNCIS that contains $B$, then for any $k$-tightest neighborhood closure invariant set $C$ that contains $B$, the following relationship holds.
\[
A\subseteq C
\]
In other words, $A$ is the smallest $k$-tightest neighborhood invariant set that contains $B$.
\end{property}

\begin{definition}[\textbf{Absolutely Distance Dividable, ADD}]
	A dataset $X$ is said as absolutely distance dividable, that is, there exists a threshold $d$ and set 
	\begin{equation}
	E_d=(e_{ij})=\left\{
	\begin{aligned}
		||x_i-x_j|| , & \text{if}\ ||x_i-x_j||\leq d\\
		0 , & \text{otherwise}
	\end{aligned}
	\right.
	\end{equation} 
	the adjacency matrix $E_d$ is a symmetric reducible matrix and it holds 
	\begin{equation}
		P_l^T\cdots P_1^TE_dP_1\cdots P_l=\begin{pmatrix}
			X_1 & & & & \\
			& \ddots & & & \\
			& & X_i & & \\
			& & & \ddots & \\
			& & & & X_K
		\end{pmatrix}
	\end{equation}
	where $K$ is the number of branches and $X_i,i=1,\cdots,K$ are symmetric submatrices with nonzero off-diagonal elements, $P_1,\cdots,P_l$ are permutation matrices. We also call that $X_i,i=1,\cdots,K$ are prefect partition (clustering) of $X$ in the sense of ADD. 
\end{definition}

The condition of a graph being Absolutely Distance Dividable (ADD) is a very strong criterion for partitioning or clustering a graph. When a dataset meets this condition, most clustering algorithms can effectively cluster the dataset by selecting appropriate parameters and initial guesses. For example, using algorithms such as \textit{Kmeans}, \textit{DBSCAN}, or \textit{AGNES}, the resulting clusters will be completely accurate. The challenge these algorithms face is how to choose the right parameters. Of course, some parameter-free algorithms can address this issue well, such as clustering algorithms based on \textit{MST}\textsuperscript{\cite{MST-DC}}.

Typically, for a given dataset $X$, there are multiple perfect partitions. For instance, when $K=1$ and $K=m$, the perfect partitions are the dataset itself or each data point as a cluster. We need to find the perfect partition of $X$ where $1 < K < m$.

