\section{Introduction}
% Clifford 3-manifold shape; Matchgate ...


Classical computing is approaching the limits imposed by Moore’s law \cite{waldrop2016chips}. Quantum computing has emerged as a promising alternative, inspired by the concept of quantum simulation introduced by Manin and Feynman \cite{Manin1980,Feynman1982}. A landmark breakthrough was Shor’s discovery of a quantum algorithm for integer factorization, which offers exponential speedup over known classical algorithms \cite{Shor1994,Shor1997}. In the Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) era \cite{preskill2018quantum,bharti2022noisy,chen2022complexity}, several experimental demonstrations have claimed quantum advantage \cite{Arute2019,Morvan2023,Zhong2020,Zhu2021,IonQ2022,Zapata2023,Madsen2022,Kim2023}. On the other hand, numerous research groups have developed classical simulation techniques to challenge and benchmark these experiments using high-performance classical computation \cite{zhou2020limits,Pan2021,Huang2020,Gray2021,Clifford2018,Oh2021,Wu2021,Moylett2019,Tindall2023,Begusic2023,Anand2023,shao2024,Chan2024}.

%quantum AI\cite{Shor_1997, Grover1996, Harrow_2009, Rebentrost_2014, lloyd2013, DunjkoPRL, biamonte2017quantum, Abbas_2021, liu2021rigorous, gao2022enhancing, zhang2024csscbl, gyurik2024expsepcq, zhao2024entanglementinducedprovablerobustquantum, zhang2024quantumclassical, ye2025, Herrmann_2022, Saggio_2021, liu2025practicalquantumfederatedlearning, zhang2024quantumcontinuallearningprogrammable, Ren_2022}. 


Understanding the limitations of classical computation relative to quantum computation is a central question in quantum information science. Two well-known classes of quantum circuits that admit efficient classical simulation are Clifford circuits, which can be simulated using the stabilizer formalism \cite{gottesman1999heisenberg}, and Matchgate circuits, which correspond to noninteracting fermionic systems and are efficiently simulable via linear algebraic methods \cite{valiant2002quantum,terhal2002classical}. A further powerful approach involves the classical simulation of tensor network states with low entanglement. When the entanglement entropy scales according to an area law, as is typical for gapped ground states in one and two dimensions, tensor network methods such as Matrix Product States (MPS) and Projected Entangled Pair States (PEPS) enable efficient simulation by reducing the computational cost to the system's boundary degrees of freedom \cite{eisert2010colloquium,verstraete2008matrix,orus2014practical}.


The union of Clifford gates and Matchgates constitutes a universal gate set for quantum circuits \cite{jozsa2008matchgates}. Significant efforts have been devoted to extending classical simulation techniques for Clifford circuits to accommodate a limited number of non-Clifford, or so-called Magic, gates \cite{bravyi2005universal,bravyi2016improved,howard2014contextuality,seddon2019quantifying,bravyi2019simulation}, and to generalizing Matchgate-based simulation frameworks beyond non-interacting fermionic systems \cite{terhal2002classical,jozsa2008matchgates}. A central challenge in the field is to construct a unified classical simulation scheme capable of efficiently simulating circuits that combine both Clifford and Matchgate operations, ideally within polynomial time and space complexity.


In this work, we resolve this challenge by a unified classical simulation framework based on the Quon language \cite{liu2017quon}, which we call \textbf{Quon Classical Simulation (QCS)}. Within this framework, we propose the concept of \textbf{magic holes} to characterize the essential source of exponential computational difficulty in QCS, see Fig.~\ref{fig:QCS complexity}. Notably, both Clifford circuits and Matchgate circuits contain no magic holes and are thus efficiently simulable in our model. Magic holes reflect global properties of a quantum circuit, and their total number is governed by topological entanglement entropy, rather than conventional entanglement entropy. This insight motivates new global reduction strategies. To formalize this idea, we introduce the concept of a \textbf{topological tensor network}, in which magic holes are represented as topological spins and the network encodes their interactions. We further develop new skein relations and reduction algorithms aimed at minimizing the number of magic holes. This methodology significantly accelerates QCS-based simulations. Overall, our framework unifies the key structural principles underlying the classical simulability of Clifford circuits, Matchgate circuits, and low-rank entanglement, thereby enabling efficient classical simulation across a broad class of quantum circuits.


