\section{Quon Classical Simulation}
\input{odd}
\input{evengraph}
\input{even}

Having established the reduction procedures for both odd holes and even Clifford holes, we are now ready to present the \text{Quon Classical Simulation (QCS)} algorithm. 
This algorithm serves as the central computational framework of our work, unifying the classical simulation of Clifford circuits and Matchgate tensor networks under the same topological language.

The QCS algorithm proceeds in three fundamental steps, each designed to systematically reduce the topological complexity of a given 2d-CDM:

Step 1: Component identification and layer construction.

Given a 2d-CDM, identify all connected components and resolve the link structure between them. Then, insert a contractible circle and re-pair charges to this circle to define the string layers.

Step 2: Odd hole elimination.

If an odd hole is present in the diagram, apply the handle-slide transformation described in Lemma~\ref{handleslideslem}. Repeat this step until no odd holes remain.

Step 3: Even Clifford hole reduction.

If all remaining holes are even, search for a Clifford cut. Apply Theorem~\ref{even} to perform the reduction.

If the reduction falls under Case~\eqref{C1}, new odd holes are introduced, and we return to Step 2.

If Case~\eqref{C2} occurs, no further action is required for that cut.

For open tensor networks with time boundaries, the same QCS process applies. Given tensors $T_1$ and $T_2$, applying QCS yields their respective simplified forms $Q(T_1)$ and $Q(T_2)$, consisting of layers and even holes. After contraction, QCS is applied again to the result, continuing the simplification process.

After completing the QCS procedure, the original 2d-CDM is reduced to one containing only magic holes, which are the sole remaining sources of exponential complexity.

To proceed, we apply a genus cut (Prop~\ref{stringgenusn}) to each magic hole. Each such cut introduces a binary variable with the price of doubling the number of diagrams. The final form consists of $2^k$ 2d-CDM's:

$$
\Gamma_i, i=1,\cdots,2^k
$$
where $k$ is the number of magic holes.

Each resulting $\Gamma_i$ is a 2d-CDM containing only strings, braids, non-braiding crossings, and charges — precisely a Matchgate Quon diagram.

By Section~\ref{qrep}, the value of each such diagram can be computed efficiently using the FKT method. This leads us to the central result of this work:

% TODO tensor contraction preprocessing
\begin{theorem}
\label{mainthm}
    Let $Q$ be a closed 2d-CDM. Then its value is given by
    \begin{equation}
    \label{maineq}
    Z(Q) =\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{|O|+|C_1|+\frac{|C_2|}{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{2^k}
    \text{Pfaffian}\left(\mathrm{Adj}[F(\widehat{\Gamma}_i(S;C))] \right)
    \end{equation}
    where:
    \begin{itemize}
    \item
    $O$ is the set of odd holes eliminated via handle slides (Lemma~\ref{handleslideslem}),
    \item
    $C_1$ and $C_2$ are the sets of even Clifford holes eliminated via Theorem~\ref{even}, corresponding respectively to cases~\eqref{C1} and~\eqref{C2},
    \item
    $k$ is the number of magic holes after QCS,
    \item
    $\Gamma_i(S;C)$ denotes the $i$-th genus-cut diagram,
    \item
    $F(\hat{\cdot})$ is the translation from a Matchgate Quon diagram to a graph-theoretic representation in section \ref{matchmain}, and
    \item
    $\mathrm{Adj}(G)$ denotes the adjacency matrix of the graph $G$ under a Pfaffian orientation as constructed by the FKT algorithm.
    \end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

