\section{Summary}
The 3D Quon Language is a 2+1 dimensional topological quantum field theory (TQFT) with space-time boundary in Alterfold theory \cite{alter2307}\cite{alter2312}.
Cliffords reduction essentially relies on surgery theory of 3D bulk manifolds.  Matchgates reduction essentially relies on Yang-Baxter relations (Reidmester moves)  of string-decorations on the 2D space boundary. These two reduction processes are parallel. 
After the two reductions in QCS, we obtain a diagram of magical holes surrounded by several layers of planar graphs. The computational complexity is $2^\#$, where $\#$ is the number of magic holes, captured by topological entanglement entropy of the space-boundary surface.
We consider this diagram as a topological tensor network, whose topological spins are magical holes. Moreover, magic holes are connected if they are surrounded by a common layer. 
We further introduce several relations to reduce the number of magic holes.
We construct one family of quantum circuits with $O(n)$ width and depth, $(O^{n^2})$ Clifford gates and Matchgates. The sampling process of the outcome states are efficiently classical simulable or not depending on the choice of the measurement basis. In the case that the qubit measurements are alternating in Puali $X$ and $Z$ basis, the efficiently classical simulable property is derived from the magic hole free move in Equ.~\ref{Equ: magic hole free move} relying on the global property of the quantum circuit. 


