\subsubsection{UnScientify vs. Former Rule-Based Systems}

% UnScientify’s integration of linguistic features makes it more flexible and effective than former rule-based systems that mostly utilized token level identification \citep{omero_writers_2020} or regex-based system \citep{chapman_simple_2001, HARKEMA2009839, atanassova_studying_2018}. While the token level detection approach struggle to accurately identify scientific uncertainty expressions due to their inability to capture the broader syntactic and semantic relationships within a sentence, the regex-based system relies solely on character-based pattern matching. For example, regex could be used to match patterns by hard-coding variations of cues with wildcards, but have difficulty with more complex variations that require an understanding of word-level features such as lemmas, parts of speech or syntactic roles.

UnScientify’s integration of linguistic features makes it more flexible and effective than former rule-based systems that mostly utilized token level identification \citep{omero_writers_2020} or regex-based system \citep{chapman_simple_2001, HARKEMA2009839, atanassova_studying_2018}. Token-level detection approaches struggle to accurately identify scientific uncertainty expressions because they fail to capture broader syntactic and semantic relationships within a sentence. Similarly, regex-based systems rely solely on character-based pattern matching, lacking the ability to parse grammatical dependencies, which further limits their effectiveness in detecting linguistic nuances.

In contrast, UnScientify is capable of processing complex sentence structures involving dependency relationships. This includes the identification of uncertainty expressions in embedded clauses or across non-contiguous spans. As was found by \cite{peng2017negbiohighperformancetoolnegation}, using dependency to detect negation or uncertainty expression in radiology reports made the detection 9.5~\% higher in precision and 5.1~\% higher in F1-score compared to using a regex-based approach.

% Consider the following phrases:
% \begin{quote}
%     \textit{"It \textbf{was somehow debatable} how X affects Y"}
% \end{quote} 
% and 

% \begin{quote}
%     \textit{"It \textbf{is somewhat debatable whether} A impacts B."}
% \end{quote}

% Regex would require hard-coding every possible variation of auxiliary verbs like \textit{"was"} or \textit{"is"} and adverbs like \textit{"somehow"} or \textit{"somewhat"} leading to inefficiency and fragility. In contrast, UnScientify, by employing a pattern matcher built on linguistic features, can seamlessly recognize such variations. It understands that \textit{"was"} and \textit{"is"} are auxiliary verbs (aux) and that \textit{"somehow"} and \textit{"somewhat"} are adverbs (advmod), allowing it to capture the similar adverbs like \textit{"highly"} or \textit{"substantially"} and generalize the detection without explicitly coding for every combination. This ability to operate on word-level features, such as lemmas and grammatical roles, makes UnScientify significantly more adaptable and precise.

