The standard Krylov subspace (\ref{Krylov}) used in traditional moment-matching requires the system matrix $\mathbf{A}$ containing the conductances to be nonsingular, which is always the case for solvable and physically viable circuits. However, the EKS and AEKS procedures of Algorithms \ref{eksm_alg} and \ref{ueks_alg} also require  the matrix 
