\subsubsection{Multi-Exit Training}

\\ Exit layers are either trained separately or jointly with the backbone network.

\paragraph{Joint Training} The joint loss is generally defined as a weighted sum of the losses of each exit and the complete network (i.e., the last-layer), which can be expressed as:

\begin{equation} 
L(\hat{y}_1,\ldots,\hat{y}_N, y;\theta) = \sum_{n=1}^{N} w_n \cdot L(\hat{y}_n,y;\theta) 
\end{equation}

where $y_N$ is the last-layer output (i.e., $y_N$ = $y_{full}$), $ L(\hat{y}_{n},y;\theta)$ is the loss for the $n^{th}$ exit-layer (typically cross-entropy loss), and $w_n$ is the relative weighting of exit-layer $n$. This joint optimization can result in improved performance for both the exit- and last-layers. However, this is not guaranteed as the accumulation of gradients from the various exits can negatively affect the convergence of the backbone network, as well as interfere with one another \cite{ee2, gao3, wei_chai}. Consequently, the relative weightings $w_i$ of the exit layers should be designed to encourage reusable features between exits \cite{ee5}. Assigning more weight to initial exit-layers promotes improved discriminative feature learning in these layers. This can improve overall network energy consumption as well as the accuracy of later exit layers via regularization \cite{gao3}. DynExit introduces a loss-weight adjustment strategy for ResNets, which adaptively modifies the weight ratio of different exits to balance accuracy and energy consumption \cite{dynexit}.
\paragraph{Separate Training}
The independent separate training of the backbone network and attached exit layers in different phases enables better extensibility and versatility towards different use-cases/tasks. The pre-trained backbone network, including its last-layer, is usually frozen while the attached exits are trained, ensuring that its performance remains unaffected. Exits can be placed at arbitrary positions in the network and trained in parallel, leaving various configuration options for deployment. However, this approach can be restrictive in terms of network optimization, potentially resulting in reduced accuracy compared to a jointly-trained equivalent. The exit-layer loss can be expressed as follows:

\begin{equation} 
    L(\hat{y}1,\ldots,\hat{y}_{N-1}, y;\theta) = \sum_{n = 1}^{N-1} w_n \cdot L(\hat{y}_n,y;\theta) 
\end{equation} 
where the backbone network remains frozen.

\paragraph{Last-Layer Distillation Training}
\label{section:lldt}

Employing Knowledge Distillation (KD) \cite{kd} between the last-layer and the various intermediary exit-layers of a network in a self-supervised manner can be used to boost exit-layer performance \cite{gao3, distill1, distill2, distill3, distill4}. The KD loss for the $n^{th}$ exit is generally defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
 L(\hat{y}_n, y;\theta) = w_n \cdot L(\hat{y}_n,y;\theta) + (1 - w_n)\cdot\text{KLD}_n   
\end{equation}
where $L(\hat{y}_n,y;\theta)$ is the loss between the $n^{th}$ exit-layer and the last-layer, and $\text{KLD}_n$ is the Kullback-Leibler divergence. \
