% In most instances, the gas-fired plants (`existing gas', `CCGT', and `CCGT-CCS') replace the offshore and storage capacity in the power-only case. The generation of

% For 80\% goal and across all scenarios, the capacity of renewables is larger in the power system when the planner allows carbon trading with the gas system. In contrast, the 95\% instances retain more of the existing gas plant capacity at the expense of offshore wind and storage.  The impact of less renewable capacity in the power-only instances is evident in the generation difference plot, Fig.~\ref{fig:pow-vs-powgas}b, in which the power-only system has more generation from gas-fired CCGT-CCS plants and less from renewables and nuclear generators. Accordingly, the cost of purchasing gas to gas generators is higher in the power-only case. Instead, the fixed cost (FOM) and the cost of investment in generators and storage ('Power/storage inv+fix') are higher in the joint power-gas planning due to higher renewable adoption. Among DRO paradigms, the MDRO tends to have higher capacity and generation difference compared to WDRO, especially in 80\% target. Overall, 

% in main outputs primarily is driven by decarbonization capacity of the generation portfolio depends on the decarbonization target. At 80\%, the joint planning model favors VRE generators and storage at the expense of CCGT-CCS, whereas at 95\% decarbonization target, the power-only model deploys higher offshore wind and storage but retains fewer existing gas generators, especially at higher electrification levels. At 80\%, the carbon budget for the power system is higher than its allocated amount in the joint system, leading to higher gas-fired plants, lower investment in VREs, and eventually significantly lower total power system cost. Conversely, the carbon budget is lower under 95\% emissions reduction goal for the power-only model, thus the model has to rely more on offshore wind, abated gas-fired plants, and storage which eventually drives up the total cost.  The results in this section highlight the importance of joint power-gas planning as ignoring the interdependency between two energy systems can lead to misleading insights. 


