\subsection{Impact of Demand and Supply Uncertainty}

% This section evaluates the value of planning under uncertainty with regard to their deterministic counterparts. 
% Deterministic models are widely used in the literature to provide insights into future infrastructure outcomes despite their shortcomings in capturing uncertainty in key problem parameters. Two factors contribute to deterministic models' popularity: i) the uncertainty of key parameters can be partially captured by data-driven and more accurate prediction models that can provide the most likely scenario; ii) higher computational tractability which allows these models to provide system outcomes in a reasonable amount of time. In case of multiple possible scenarios, the planner can use deterministic models to evaluate outcomes for each scenario independently (Monte-Carlo method). 

Often time the literature overlooks the uncertainty in all the key parameters affected by weather variations (see Table~\ref{tab:lit-review}). 
We carry out experiments to highlight the importance of uncertainty in both supply and demand parameters and their impact on key energy infrastructure planning outcomes of interest. Specifically, the DRO and SP models are evaluated for three cases where uncertainty is modeled as follows: a) uncertainty only in supply parameters, i.e. VRE capacity factors (S1D0), b) uncertainty in electricity and gas demands only (S0D1), and c) uncertainty in both supply and demand parameters (S1D1). 


