This paper studies the trade-off between privacy guarantee, model convergence, and performance distribution fairness of privacy-preserving PFL, more specifically, DP-Ditto. 
% While FL is unaffected by personalized model training, it depends on the outcome of the FL. The clients' concern about their privacy and the consequent perturbation of their local models can affect the convergence and fairness of personalized model training.
We analyze the convergence upper bound of the personalized learning (PL) and accordingly optimize the aggregation number of FL given a privacy budget. We also analyze the fairness of PL in DP-Ditto on a class of linear problems, revealing the possibility of maximizing the fairness of privacy-preserving PFL given a privacy budget and aggregation number.
{
% Base on the analysis, a joint design for balancing PFL convergence and fairness with guantanteed privacy-preserving is designed.
 To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work that investigates the trade-off among the privacy, convergence, and fairness of PFL, and optimizes PFL for convergence under the constraints of performance distribution fairness and privacy requirements.}
The major contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item 
We propose a new privacy-preserving PFL framework, i.e., DP-Ditto, by incorporating an $(\epsilon,\delta)$-DP perturbation mechanism into Ditto.
This extension is non-trivial and necessitates a delicate balance between convergence, privacy, and fairness.
\item 
A convergence upper bound of DP-Ditto is derived, capturing the impact of DP on the convergence of personalized models. 
The number of global aggregations is identified by minimizing the convergence upper bound.  
%analyzing the minimum of the upper bound to specify the region for an efficient one-dimensional search. 
\item 
We analyze the fairness of DP-Ditto on a class of linear problems to reveal the conditional existence and uniqueness of the optimal setting balancing convergence and fairness, given a privacy requirement. 
% and derive the optimal \textcolor{red}{weighting coefficient} to maximize fairness. 
% { This is achieved by analyzing the distribution of the DP-perturbed global model and optimal personalized model, and differs substantially from the fairness analysis of Ditto.} 
% analyze the interplay between the optimal weighting coefficient and DP noise, and 
%We illustrate the joint optimization of DP-Ditto for convergence and fairness.
\end{itemize}
%Extensive simulations show that LoIU-based resource allocation improves task reliability by about 33\%, 44\%, and 40\%, compared to AoI-based, Age of Incorrect Information (AoII)-based, and Urgency of Information (UoI)-based resource allocation. The proposed MADRL-based method effectively improves the freshness and utility of information by up to 98\%, compared to these alternatives.  

Extensive experiments validate our convergence and fairness analysis of DP-Ditto and the obtained optimal FL aggregation number and weighting coefficients of FL versus PL. Three image classification tasks are performed using deep neural network (DNN), multi-class linear regression (MLR), and convolutional neural network (CNN) on the Federated MNIST, Federated FMNIST, and Federated CIFAR10 datasets. 
% The analytically identified optimal number of global aggregations and the interplay between the optimal \textcolor{red}{weighting coefficient} and DP noise are consistent with experimental observations, validating our convergence and fairness analysis. 
DP-Ditto can outperform its benchmarks, i.e., the DP-perturbed FedAMP~\cite{huang2021personalized}, pFedMe~\cite{t2020personalized}, APPLE~\cite{luo2022adapt}, and FedALA~\cite{zhang2023fedala}, by $99.98\%$, $32.71\%$, $97.04\%$, and $99.72\%$, respectively, in fairness and $59.06\%$, $9.66\%$, $28.67\%$, and $64.31\%$ in accuracy.

