For real-world robotic applications, HOVER \cite{he2025hover} introduces a multi-mode policy distillation framework that allows humanoid robots to switch seamlessly between tasks like locomotion, manipulation, and navigation using a single unified policy distilled from an oracle. This eliminates the need for task-specific controllers, demonstrating general-purpose control in real-world environments, akin to the versatility seen in MaskedMimic for virtual characters.

All the above methods follow the idea of BFM, which is revealed from two aspects: (i) their ability to learn a broad behavior coverage from diverse data sources, and (ii) their fast adaptation ability to downstream tasks. These models are trained on large-scale datasets and can be generalized across a wide range of motor skills, such as locomotion, HOI, and task-specific behaviors, without being limited to a single task. For instance, InterMimic is capable of handling various HOT tasks, while MoConVQ adapts to different tasks, such as goal-reaching and text-conditioned motion generation. Additionally, these models exhibit fast adaptation to new tasks, with minimal retraining, demonstrating their capacity to apply learned behaviors to new and unseen scenarios. Thus, the broad behavior coverage combined with the adaptation ability collectively characterizes these methods as BFMs.

\subsubsection{Intrinsic Reward-driven Learning}

In tracking-based learning, the agent is consistently provided with an explicit objective (\textit{e.g.}, joint angles or velocities) and trained via a well-specified reward function to achieve targeted skill acquisition. In contrast, intrinsic reward-driven learning presents a distinct approach, where the agent is motivated to explore the environment without relying on explicit task-specific rewards. Instead, the agent is guided by intrinsic rewards, which are self-generated signals that encourage exploration, skill acquisition, or novelty detection. Extensive strategies for intrinsic reward-driven learning have been developed, including curiosity-driven exploration \cite{pathak2017curiosity,burda2018exploration,pathak2019self,sekar2020planning}, skill discovery \cite{gregor2017variational,eysenbach2018diversity,hansen2020fast,liu2021aps}, and maximizing data coverage \cite{bellemare2016unifying,ostrovski2017count,seo2021state,yarats2021reinforcement}, each encouraging the agent to explore different aspects of the environment. Denote by $I:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ the intrinsic reward function, the optimization objective in Eq.~(\ref{eq:rl}) is reformulated as
\begin{equation}
    J_{\pi}=\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}\big(r(\bm{s}_t)+\beta_t\cdot I(\bm{s}_t)\big)\right],
\end{equation}
where $\beta_t=\beta_0(1-\kappa)^t$ is the exploration coefficient controlling the relative weight of intrinsic motivation over time, and $\kappa$ is a decay rate.

