\section{Introduction}
Databases provide efficient mechanisms for storing, organizing, and retrieving data. Query languages such as SQL (for relational databases), SPARQL (for RDF graphs), or Cypher (for graph databases) enable users to interact with these systems~\cite{hogan2021knowledge}.  %to store data and extract meaningful insights~\cite{hogan2021knowledge}. 
Advancements in large language models (LLMs) have enabled the translation of natural language questions into database queries (Text2SQL, Text2SPARQL, Text2Cypher), making databases more accessible to non-experts. 
% For this purpose, either general-purpose foundational LLMs or specifically fine-tuned models can be utilized with appropriate prompts. 
However, most research in this area has focused on English, with limited attention given to other languages~\cite{jannuzzi2024zero, geng2024not}. 

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/text2cypher_v2.png}
    \caption{The user wants to generate a Cypher query for the question `What are the movies of Tom Hanks`. However, they may express the same question in different languages, such as English (EN), Spanish (ES) or Turkish (TR). 
    % A Text2Cypher model translates the natural language input into the corresponding Cypher query, i.e., `MATCH (actor:Person \{name: "Tom Hanks"\})-[:ACTED\_IN]->(movie:Movie) RETURN movie.title`
    }
  \label{fig:text2cypher}
\end{figure}

This work focuses on Text2Cypher, converting natural language questions into executable Cypher queries (see Figure~\ref{fig:text2cypher}). For example, a user might want to write a Cypher query for the question \textit{"What are the movies of Tom Hanks?"}, which can be expressed in different languages, such as English (EN), Spanish (ES), or Turkish (TR). 
Regardless of language, a Text2Cypher model should generate the same query, such as \textit{"MATCH (actor:Person \{name: "Tom Hanks"\})-[:ACTED\_IN]->(movie:Movie) RETURN movie.title"}. % to retrieve relevant data. 
%In order to broaden accessibility and inclusiveness, a Text2Cypher system should support inputs from different languages. 
% Hypothesis: If we want users to interact naturally with databases, why must we restrict them to English? 
Furthermore, we aim to evaluate multilingual performance of the foundational LLMs on this task. For this purpose, we construct a test set of semantically aligned questions in English, Spanish, and Turkish, all paired with the same Cypher query. This setup enables cross-lingual benchmarking, isolating the impact of input language on model performance. 
Our main contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item We release a multilingual version of the Text2Cypher test set~\cite{ozsoy2025text2cypher}, translated into Spanish and Turkish to enable cross-lingual evaluation\footnote{Dataset: \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/mgoNeo4j/translated_text2cypher24_testset}}.. These languages were chosen for their linguistic diversity and resource differences. 
% The dataset is publicly available\footnote{Dataset: \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/mgoNeo4j/translated_text2cypher24_testset}}.

\item We evaluate multiple foundational models on this dataset using standardized prompts and metrics, comparing performance across English, Spanish, and Turkish with semantically equivalent questions.

\item We analyze the impact of language on Text2Cypher performance. Our results show that models perform best on English, then Spanish, and lowest in Turkish. We attribute the lower performance in Turkish to its agglutinative structure and inguistic distance from Indo-European languages.
\end{itemize}

