\section{Related Work} \label{rel_work}

Large language models (LLMs) have become central to a wide range of natural language processing tasks. While many early LLMs were trained primarily on English data, recently their capabilities are extended to other languages through the development of multilingual models~\cite{nicholas2023lost}. Researchers have also begun exploring the internal mechanisms of these models~\cite{zhao2024llama, kargaran2024mexa, zhong2024beyond, schut2025multilingual} and evaluating their cross-lingual performance in various application domains~\cite{ozsoy2024multilingual, jannuzzi2024zero}. 
Multilingual models are trained on datasets that include multiple languages, allowing them to learn cross-lingual representations. This enables them to use word associations and grammatical patterns learned from high-resource languages, such as English, and apply them to languages with less available training data. However, because English still dominates most training sets, these models may also transfer linguistic patterns and assumptions specific to English into other language contexts~\cite{nicholas2023lost, zhao2024llama}. As a result, LLMs perform significantly better in higher-resource languages and those similar to them compared to lower-resource languages~\cite{nicholas2023lost}. 

In the context of translating natural language to database query languages, much of the multilingual work has focused on the Text2SQL task~\cite{dou2023multispider, jose2021mrat}. Recent studies have considered languages such as Portuguese~\cite{jannuzzi2024zero, pedroso2025performance}, Arabic~\cite{almohaimeed2024ar}, Russian~\cite{bakshandaeva2022pauq}, and Turkish~\cite{kanburoglu2024turspider}. 
In this work, we study the Text2Cypher task, which translates natural language questions into Cypher queries. 
%We prepare dataset in English, Spanish, and Turkish and evaluate the performance of foundational LLMs across these languages. 

