\subsection{Dataset Preparation}
We used the publicly available Text2Cypher dataset~\cite{ozsoy2025text2cypher}, which includes English questions, database schema, ground-truth Cypher queries, and metadata. 
In order to evaluate foundational models across languages, we translated the question field of the test split into Spanish and Turkish using an LLM, and compared results across all three languages. 
English is an extremely high-resource Indo-European language, Spanish is a high-resource Indo-European language, and Turkishis a medium-resource Altaic language with distinct linguistic traits~\cite{joshi2020state, nicholas2023lost}. 
% Given these characteristics, we expect slightly lower performance for Spanish and a more significant drop for Turkish due to its linguistic divergence. 
Based on this, we expect slightly lower performance for Spanish and a larger drop for Turkish due to linguistic differences. 
We used the following steps for translation:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Masking Named Entities and Quotes}: To ensure that named entities and quoted text remained unchanged during translation, we first masked them. Named entities were replaced with placeholders indicating their type and index (e.g., LOCATION\_0), while quoted strings were replaced with QUOTE\_<index> markers. For example, the sentence "Hello, I work at 'Neo4j' in London" was masked as "Hello, I work at QUOTE\_0 in LOCATION\_0". % We used spaCy for this purpose.

    \item \textbf{Translation Using an LLM}: We translated the masked questions using the GPT-4o-mini model, guided by the prompt shown in Table~\ref{tab:translate_prompt}. % version around: May 10-20, 2025.

    \item \textbf{Restoring Masks}: After translation, we replaced the placeholders with their original named entities and quoted expressions to reconstruct the final question.
\end{itemize}

% The resulting dataset, containing questions in English, Spanish, and Turkish, is made publicly available. 
Note that the ground-truth Cypher queries were not translated. These queries contain (i) Cypher-specific syntax (e.g., \textit{MATCH, WHERE}), (ii) terms from the schema (e.g., \textit{Person, ACTED\_IN, Movie}), and (iii) user-provided literals (e.g., \textit{"Tom Hanks"}), all of which are intended to remain in their original form.

