\section{Conclusion} \label{conc}
This work presents a performance comparison of foundational LLMs on the Text2Cypher task across English, Spanish, and Turkish. We created and released a multilingual test set by translating English questions into Spanish and Turkish while keeping the same ground-truth queries, enabling fair cross-lingual comparison of model performance. 
Our results consistently show that foundational models perform best on English questions, followed by Spanish, and lowest on Turkish. This pattern is likely driven by differences in training data availability and the linguistic characteristics, which pose additional challenges for the models. 
We also explored the impact of translating task instruction prompts alongside the input questions. Our findings suggest that prompt translation slightly improves translation-based evaluation but has little to no effect on query execution results, suggesting that prompt language has minimal impact on overall performance in our setting. 
Future work includes schema localization to improve multilingual performance, fine-tuning models on individual or combined languages and  expanding evaluation to additional languages.

