\begin{abstract}
% Although development engineers can easily discover a large number of vulnerabilities with the help of vulnerability detection tools, addressing these vulnerabilities without discernment can result in superfluous development expenses, as a significant number of identified vulnerabilities present minimal risk of being exploited or would have negligible effects in practical situations.
% %
% Therefore, evaluating the severity of vulnerabilities is becoming increasingly necessary and crucial for improving the efficiency of software development.
% Existing vulnerability assessment methods are based on source code with corresponding manually derived descriptions.
% Due to the uneven professional capabilities of code auditors, the performance of these vulnerability assessment methods is extremely limited by the quality of vulnerability descriptions.

Although modern vulnerability detection tools enable developers to efficiently identify numerous security flaws, indiscriminate remediation efforts often lead to superfluous development expenses. 
This is particularly true given that a substantial portion of detected vulnerabilities either possess low exploitability or would incur negligible impact in practical operational environments. 
Consequently, vulnerability severity assessment has emerged as a critical component in optimizing software development efficiency.
Existing vulnerability assessment methods typically rely on manually crafted descriptions associated with source code artifacts. 
However, due to variability in description quality and subjectivity in intention interpretation, the performance of these methods is seriously limited.
% Although evaluating the severity of vulnerabilities is becoming increasingly necessary and crucial for improving the efficiency of software development,  existing vulnerability assessment solutions heavily depend on fixed rules without understanding the code contexts of vulnerabilities, resulting in inaccurate evaluations.
%
%
To address this issue, this paper introduces VulStamp, a novel intention-guided framework, to facilitate description-free vulnerability assessment.
% To address this issue, this paper introduces VulStamp, a novel intention-guided framework to facilitate description-free vulnerability assessment using Large Language Models (LLMs) and provide actionable repair suggestions.
Specifically, VulStamp adopts static analysis together with Large Language Model (LLM) to extract the intention information of vulnerable code.
Based on the intention information, VulStamp uses a prompt-tuned model for vulnerability assessment.
Furthermore, to mitigate the problem of imbalanced data associated with vulnerability types, VulStamp integrates a Reinforcement Learning (RL)-based prompt-tuning method to train the assessment model. 
% the training of the code reviewer model.
% we develop a gradient-enhanced model training scheme to mitigate the problem of imbalanced data associated with vulnerability types, thus facilitating a more precise suggestion-supplied vulnerability evaluation. 
% introduces VulStamp, a novel intention-driven framework aimed at accurately assessing software vulnerabilities and providing actionable repair suggestions.
%
% By using our proposed intention-guided data processing approach, VulStamp can extract syntax-related information regarding root causes and effects of potential bugs from the provided source code.
%
% Meanwhile, based on our designed prompt template, VulEval employs Large Language Models (LLMs) to generate a report for each identified vulnerability, detailing aspects such as the exploitability, impact, and scope of the vulnerability. 
%
% Taking advantage of the synergy between these two techniques, we develop a gradient-enhanced model training scheme to mitigate the problem of imbalanced data associated with vulnerability types, thus facilitating a more precise suggestion-supplied vulnerability evaluation. 
%
% Extensive experimental results on well-known LLMs and vulnerability benchmarks reveal that VulEval surpasses state-of-the-art methods by not only offering superior vulnerability assessment performance, but also delivering high-quality suggestions for vulnerability repair.
Extensive experimental results demonstrate that {\tool} outperforms the state-of-the-art baselines by an average of
12.9\%, 102.6\%, 18.3\%, and 54.1\% in terms of AUC, precision, recall and F1-score, respectively.





\end{abstract}


\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Software vulnerability assessment, common vulnerabilities and exposures, intention, LLM, prompt tuning.
\end{IEEEkeywords}


