\section{DATASET}
\label{sec:dataset}
We use a dataset of 318 PE therapy sessions (see Table~\ref{tab:audio_stats}) recorded at Emory University, each lasting 60–90 minutes and capturing therapist-client interactions. All data were handled within IRB-approved environments, with no PII leaving secure storage. We annotated key protocol elements—\textit{orientation} (P1), \textit{imaginal exposure} (P2), and \textit{processing} (P3)—which typically occur sequentially during a session (Figure~\ref{fig:session_split_up}), motivating our timestamp prediction task. To minimize manual labeling, we developed a zero-shot LLM pipeline that extracts protocol presence and timestamp boundaries from transcripts in JSON format. To ensure scalable validation, 10\% (30 sessions) were reviewed by trained raters, yielding 94.4\% timestamp accuracy (within 5–10 seconds) and 97.7\% label accuracy. This validated our approach without requiring extensive expert clinician time. Audio was downsampled from 44.1–48 kHz to 16 kHz, normalized, and converted to \texttt{.wav}. Transcripts with sentence-level timestamps and speaker labels were generated using Amazon HealthScribe. After excluding sessions over 1.5 hours (due to transcription limits), those with alignment issues, or lacking valid labels for all protocol elements, we retained 308 recordings. The dataset was split into 216 training, 45 validation, and 47 test sessions, with balanced representation of therapy phases.

