\subsection{Good Approximation Guarantee}
Lemma~\ref{lem:twoextreme} shows that the population will always contain $0^n$ and $1^n$ once they are reached for the first time. From basic calculations based on Algorithm~\ref{alg:normalization}, we obtain the following clear form for the normalized function value when the population contains both $0^n$ and $1^n$.
% \wj{(Delete)}Hence, in the following, we assume that both $0^n$ and $1^n$ are contained in the population. The first fact after this assumption is that the normalized function value has a clear form as in the follow lemma. It is just a pure calculation based on Algorithm~\ref{alg:normalization}.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:norm}
Consider using the \NSGAT with population size $N$ to optimize \omm with problem size $n$. Let $N < n + 1$ and $N_r\le N$. Assume that the two extremal points $0^n$ and $1^n$ are included in the population. Then for any individual $x$ in the population, its function value $f(x)$ is normalized to $f^n(x) = \frac{1}{n}f(x)$.
\end{lemma}
